 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Wave Divergence:** Standard depth peeling suffers from high wave divergence because lighting and depth finding are combined, leading to shader discards. This method avoids this by separating depth finding (Step 1) from shading (Step 3) [S1].
-*   **Surface Intersection Check:** In Step 1, it is critical to check for intersection with the encoded surface. Without this, transparent parts would occupy each layer, requiring more passes for a correct image [S1].
-*   **Performance Scaling:** Empirical results show that 3-4 passes are often sufficient. After the second pass, there are almost no changes in the resulting image [S1].
-*   **Resolution Impact:** Performance is better at smaller resolutions due to cache utilization. At larger resolutions, cache hit rates decrease, but the method still outperforms basic shell mapping without LODs [S1].
+*   **Wave Divergence:** Standard depth peeling suffers from high wave divergence because lighting and depth finding are combined, leading to shader discards. This method separates them, minimizing divergence [S1].
+*   **Surface Intersection Check:** In Step 1, it is mandatory to check for intersection with the encoded surface. Failure to do so results in transparent parts occupying every layer, significantly increasing the number of required passes [S1].
+*   **Performance Scaling:** Empirical results indicate that 3-4 passes are sufficient in many cases. After the second pass, there are almost no changes in the resulting image, and rendering time reduces with each subsequent pass [S1].
+*   **Hardware Restrictions:** The method cannot simply copy a texture into the hardware z-buffer; it requires a shader pass to convert the depth, due to hardware restrictions [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of passes (layers) and $P$ is the number of pixels. Each pass involves rendering the geometry once for depth finding, a full-screen pass for depth conversion, rendering the geometry again for shading, and another full-screen pass for opacity culling. Thus, 4 draw calls per layer.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(P)$ for the color buffer, opacity buffer, and z-buffer. The geometry storage is independent of the OIT method.
-*   **Measured Performance:** Tested on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080 GPU at resolutions 800x800 and 2560x1600. The method is faster than basic shell mapping at larger resolutions due to reduced overdraw [S1].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Reduces fragment shader invocations by using hardware z-buffer culling.
-    *   Minimizes wave divergence by separating depth finding and shading.
-    *   Provides stable quality for the first $K$ layers.
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Requires multiple passes (4 per layer).
-    *   May suffer from color altering if the number of layers is insufficient (e.g., darkening where many prisms meet) [S1].
-    *   Not suitable for scenes requiring infinite transparency layers.
-*   **When to Use:** When rendering semi-transparent layers on animated surfaces where the first few layers are critical for visual quality, and hardware performance is a concern.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When infinite transparency layers are required, or when the scene is too simple to warrant the overhead of multiple passes.
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method performs $N$ passes, where $N$ is the number of layers (typically 3-4) [S1]. Each pass involves rendering geometry and full-screen depth conversion passes. The cost per pass decreases as more layers are culled [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the z-buffer, color buffer, and potentially temporary buffers for depth conversion [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Tested on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080 GPU at resolutions 800x800 and 2560x1600 [S1]. The method outperforms basic shell mapping on larger texture sizes due to better cache utilization and reduced overdraw [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Utilizes hardware depth test for culling, avoiding expensive shader-based discard operations. Reduces wave divergence by separating depth finding from shading.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a user-defined maximum number of layers. May exhibit color altering on parts where the surface is nearly perpendicular to the view projection plane due to not handling all layers [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Suitable for real-time rendering of small-scale volumetric structures on animated surfaces where the first $K$ semi-transparent layers are critical for visual quality [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Not appropriate for cases requiring linked list construction, transmittance approximation, or stochastic transparency, as these do not guarantee the stable quality of the first $K$ layers [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced in the paper "Real-time Rendering of Small-scale Volumetric Structure on Animated Surfaces" [S1]. It addresses the limitations of standard depth peeling, which was introduced in [15] and has advanced variants [19, 20]. The key innovation is the use of hardware z-buffer for culling instead of shader-based discard operations, which were identified as a major bottleneck in previous approaches [S1]. The method is particularly relevant for volumetric textures on animated surfaces, where sorting tetrahedrons is too expensive [S1].
+The method was introduced in the paper "Real-time Rendering of Small-scale Volumetric Structure on Animated Surfaces" [S1]. It addresses the limitations of standard depth peeling, which performs culling within shaders using discard operations dependent on texture reads, making each pass expensive and requiring re-rendering of all geometry [S1]. The technique is a modification of depth peeling, leveraging hardware z-buffer capabilities to optimize performance for animated volumetric shells [S1].
 
